WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.990
Hello, and welcome to this video on Concepts.

2
00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:14.429
In this, we are going to do an overview of the three primary components that are going to control the animation.

3
00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:20.859
That is the Controller, the Pawn, and the Animation Blueprint.

4
00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:22.859
Firstly, we have the Controller.

5
00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:28.059
It's like a central command unit that steers the behaviour of Pawns.

6
00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:32.289
Pawns are characters or objects within the game world.

7
00:00:32.289 --> 00:00:37.090
Now, this can work by either interpreting the player input

8
00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.969
or it could be controlled by an AI.

9
00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:45.679
So the computer would control it using something like a behaviour tree.

10
00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:52.079
Next, we have the Pawn, which is the physical representation of the character in the game.

11
00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:59.219
It is vital for defining how a character moves and interacts with the game's physics.

12
00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:06.579
Finally, we have the Animation Blueprint, which is a system for animating a skeletal mesh.

13
00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:13.180
Within this, we have two main elements, the Event Graph and the Ami Graph.

14
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.540
The Event Graph plays a critical role by receiving data from other sources

15
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:25.019
and then processing that data so that it can be used to generate the animations.

16
00:01:25.019 --> 00:01:29.090
This data will then be funneled into the Ami Graph.

17
00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.250
The Ami Graph itself, short for Animation Graph,

18
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:39.019
is tasked with processing the incoming data to drive the animation,

19
00:01:39.019 --> 00:01:43.890
ensuring that the character's movements are both fluid and accurate.

20
00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:47.329
It can also use state machines and the control rig

21
00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:53.060
to produce complicated animations that respond to the gameplay.

22
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.540
Together, these components work in tandem to bring the character to life,

23
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.700
providing a seamless and interactive player experience.

24
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.939
Understanding how they interlink is crucial for any developer

25
00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:10.289
looking to set up a professional-looking game animation.

26
00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.729
Let's go through the animation process step by step so that we can see how they connect.

27
00:02:15.729 --> 00:02:21.650
So firstly, we have the Pawn, or character, which carries a skeletal mesh.

28
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.969
This mesh is the framework that defines the physical structure

29
00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.689
and appearance of the character in the game world.

30
00:02:29.979 --> 00:02:36.139
Secondly, the skeletal mesh is equipped to hold and execute the Animation Blueprint.

31
00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:42.539
These Blueprints are pivotal in defining the character's movement and behavior.

32
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.960
Thirdly, the Animation Blueprint processes the Ami Graph's output.

33
00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:56.270
The Ami Graph is the engine that calculates the character's animation state frame by frame,

34
00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:00.990
based on the gameplay and the environmental interaction.

35
00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:09.360
Finally, we have the Event Graph, and that collects and deciphers data for the Ami Graph.

36
00:03:09.979 --> 00:03:13.659
It acts as a conduit, enabling the Animation Blueprint

37
00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:17.900
to respond dynamically to real-time gameplay events.

38
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.159
This is all we are going to cover for Concepts.

39
00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.900
We will be going over a lot of other elements,

40
00:03:25.979 --> 00:03:29.819
however, this is best done in the context of an exercise.

41
00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:34.060
This will allow us to see how everything fits together in context.

42
00:03:35.259 --> 00:03:40.379
So our next video, we will be starting setting up for the exercise.
